- `branch(b3), branch(b4), branch(b5)`: Chains three sequential XOR branches to evaluate order status instead of modeling a single decision point with three alternative outcomes, distorting the control-flow logic relative to the text ("If the 'Order is complete and all items were returned,' ... However, if the 'Order is incomplete and items are missing,' ... Similarly, if the 'Order is complete but items are damaged,'...") (SMC3, SMC4).
- `branch(b6)`: Incorrectly merges four distinct process segments (packing, storing, missing-item charges, damaged-item charges) into one gateway with multiple outgoing edges, contradicting the text which presents packing and storing as alternatives followed by separate conditional steps ("After this, if the 'Items are packed for an existing order,' ... Alternatively, if the 'Items are stored for future orders,' ... In case of missing items... Additionally, if there are damaged items...", SMC4).
- `precedence(b6, n17), precedence(b6, n19)`: Routes the missing and damaged item evaluation nodes directly from the first packing/storage branch, bypassing the textual chronology that positions these evaluations as subsequent conditional notifications rather than direct alternatives to packing/storing (SMC3).
- `branch(b16, AND)`: Models mutually exclusive item-disposition conditions as a concurrent parallel split, whereas the text explicitly frames them as alternatives ("If the 'Items are repaired and added into inventory,' ... On the other hand, if the 'Items can't be repaired and are discarded,'...") requiring an XOR gateway (SMC4).
- `defines(v1,...,v14)`: Declares all decision variables with empty value ranges `{}`, failing to specify valid domains (e.g., {true, false} or descriptive labels) necessary to faithfully represent the binary/multiple-choice conditions posed in the source text (SMC6).
- `actor(a_default, [...])`: Assigns every single node to one generic actor, ignoring the distinct participant responsibilities explicitly mentioned in the source ("Customer", "Delivery Crew", and implied store/system staff for counting, emailing, and inventory management) (SMC7).
- Omission of intermediate termination: The source explicitly states "Finally, when the process is complete, it ends" after the missing-items-return decision, but the model lacks a corresponding `end` node at this juncture and instead flows unconditionally into the repair/disposal steps (SMC1).

